Introduction After titanium dioxide (TiO 2 ) electrodes were used to discover photocatalytic water splitting by Nakata, & Fujishima 1 , TiO 2  has emerged as a highly desirable photocatalyst due to its many benefits, including abundance, environmental friendliness, structural stability, and affordable 2 . Although, effectively capturing solar energy for use in photoelectrochemical cells, solar cells, and photocatalysis is substantially limited by the comparatively large materials that have a band gap such as TiO 2 . As a result, a great deal of study has already been done to comprehend the underlying mechanisms and inquire about routes to enhance the photocatalytic activity and efficiency of TiO 2 3 , 4 . Thankfully, advances in the synthesis, transformation, and optimization of photocatalysts (such as TiO 2 ) have been achieved constantly to increase the absorption of ~ 50% visible light (sunlight) for use in photocatalytic processes 5 , 6 . As compare to TiO 2  there are manu oxides which showed good photocatalytic properties, but TiO 2  is considered good 7 – 13 . Generally, altering TiO 2  with an appropriate impurity not only modifies the ultraviolet radiation kinetics and mechanism but also provides an additional electromagnetic spectrum that is lacking in host semiconducting TiO 2 14 . A few techniques have been offered to increase the characteristics and photocatalytic uses of TiO 2 . One is doping to TiO 2  catalysts, which might broaden its visible light absorption capacity 15 , 16 . Second, is the synthesis of TiO 2 -based nanomaterials/thin films with specified interface configurations due to certain particular crystallographic directions, which are likely to display better catalytic properties than the rest of the heterogeneous structural features 17 . Third is a composite of other useful compounds (for example, graphene, semiconductor oxides, nanomaterials and carbon nanotubes) with TiO 2 , to achieve a beneficial interaction combining these components 18 , 19 . The main reason why TiO 2 -based materials perform better is because of the hydroxyl radicals and oxidizing holes that are produced when photoexcited electrons are present. Compared to other oxidants, these hydroxyl radicals have a greater capacity for oxidation 20 . However, Photocatalyst Electron–Hole Pairs PEHPs recombinate quickly, making it difficult to absorb visible light during the photocatalytic degradation process. The wide bandgap of TiO 2  results in low solar utilization, which limits its usefulness 21 . To overcome these limitations, a number of studies have been carried out using a variety of techniques, such as chemical doping and reduction 22 – 30 . Generally speaking, altering the TiO 2  band gap with a consistent dispersion of impurity modifies the materials electrical and structural characteristics along boosts its visibility light illumination efficiency with a feat that is not achievable with TiO 2  in its pure form 31 . Several metals, including Cr, Fe, Zn, S, Cu, Ce Mn, W, V, and Al, have been effectively doped into the host TiO 2  to promote photocatalytic behavior (PB) and the low range over which electron–hole pairs recombine via PEHP 32 – 35 . Because doping with metal can both capture electrons (photo-induced) and limit the rate of metal recombination, it has been shown to enhance the photodegradation properties of TiO 2 36 . The electrons produced during the PB encourage the defect rates caused by metal inducing, resulting in the formation of holes in the TiO 2  valence band (VB) to improve the PB 37 . Al-doped TiO 2  has been widely studied for its usage in PB, methylene blue deprivation, and hydrogen production 38 . Nonetheless, a notable obstacle is the insolubility of dopants, phase changes, thermal fluctuations, and the rise in carrier concentration centers 39 . Non-metals (Nm) like F, C, and S are induced to TiO 2  at the O side, to overcome these obstacles, and these are the most well-known photocatalysts 40 . Since Nm do not form recombination centers 41 , it has been demonstrated that their doping of TiO 2  results in improved PB activities. The TiO 2  band gap is lowered and the valence band is shifted upward by the dopants, i.e. non-metals like F, C, and S p orbitals combined with 2p orbitals of oxygen at TiO 2 . The S ionic radius is too large for the TiO 2  lattice to bind with, making S-doped TiO 2  the most promising option among them as a photocatalyst 42 – 44 . Due to the elements synergistic effect, both Nm and metals have demonstrated excellent characteristics of the visible spectrum, in contrast to the substitution of S and Nm doped TiO 2 45 . There are a few reports regarding the codoping of both metals and Nms to the TiO 2  for the best PB. The co-doping of both metals and Nms to the TiO 2  is still under discussion. Therefore, we investigated the effects of hydrothermally synthesized Al and S co-doped TiO 2  with fixed Al (2%) and varying S contents (2%, 4%, 6%, and 8%) in this paper. Through the combination of Al and S effects on the electrical and structural responses of TiO 2 , because double double-doping approach may enhance TiO 2  PB performance by controlling the physical properties, band gap adjustment, and reducing the order in which PEHPs of recombination occur 46 . The present research shows a clear relationship between the material photodegradation behavior and its structural phases. There is still a significant gap in the systematic correlation of phase transitions (anatase–rutile–brookite), bandgap modulation, and photocatalytic activity under visible-light irradiation, despite the fact that many research articles have investigated doping techniques for ZnO, SiC, graphene-doped composites and TiO 2 47 – 49 . The current study provides a thorough examination of the impacts of Al 3 ⁺ and S⁶⁺ co-doping on the structural, optical, and photocatalytic activity of TiO 2  nanostructures produced by a hydrothermal technique, in contrast to other works that primarily focus on either single metal or non-metal doping. The objective of this research is to clearly define the link between the relevant photocatalytic activity, bandgap engineering, phase transition response, and dopant concentration. With the Al content maintained at 2%, we show how co-doping regulates visible-light activity by adjusting the band structure and reducing PEHP recombination by systematically changing the S concentration (2%, 4%, 6%, and 8%).

Experimental method We purchased Aluminum (III) chloride hexahydrate (AlCl 3 .6H 12 O 6 , Purity 99.999%), titanium (III) chloride hexahydrate (TiCl 3 . 6H 2 O, Purity 99.999%), thiourea (SC(NH 2 ) 2 , 99.9%) and sodium hydroxide (NaOH, Purity 99.999%) from Alfa-Aser. Through the experiment, Alfa aser-made de-ionized water was used to synthesize the samples. First, 2 g of TiCl 3 .6H 2 O was added in one beker containing 50 ml (ml) of deionized water and starred for 30 min. Second, 0.5 g of NaOH was added to the second beaker with 20 ml of deionized water for 20 min. Then aforementioned NaOH liquid medium was conventionally added dropwise through a pipet to 50 ml of TiCl 3 . 6H 2 O solution and allowed to stand at room temperature for 10 min before being magnetically stirred. The mentioned solution was vigorously stirred for 50 min to ensure a homogenous solution. Finally, the stirred solvent was transferred to a 100 ml stainless steel Teflon line autoclave and kept for reaction under 150 °C for 24 h. The temperature of the solution in the oven was gradually raised to 150 °C. Following centrifugation, the resultant solution was repeatedly washed several times with deionized water until it reached a pH of 7. The resulting mixture was transferred into a beaker and allowed to dry for 24 h at 60 °C in an oven. Furhtermore, Aluminum nitrate nonahydrate (Al(NO₃)₃·9H 2 O) and sodium sulfate (Na 2 SO₄) were employed as sources of Al 3 ⁺/Al 2 ⁺ and S⁶⁺, respectively with the mixture of TiCl 3 .6H 2 O in one beaker containing 50 ml (ml) of deionized water and stirred for 30 min. The molar ratio of dopants to Ti was maintained at 2%. To facilitate uniform precipitation, the pH of the solution was adjusted to ~ 9 using ammonium hydroxide. The gel was dried at 100 °C for 12 h, then calcined at 500 °C for 3 h in air to achieve crystallinity and dopant inclusion. The progressive temperature increase (5 °C/min) resulted in regulated phase development. Finally, the pure TiO 2  (named = P) nanoparticles were at the bottom of the beaker. The same process was used to 2%-Al and 2% (named = X1), 4% (named = X2), 6% (named = X3), and 8% (named = X4) S co-doped TiO 2 , only the difference is the first beaker was contain TiCl 3 . 6H 2 O + AlCl 3 .6H 12 O 6+ SC(NH 2 ) 2 . The schematic diagram of Fig.  1  shows the detailed hydrothermal routes. Fig. 1 The process flow diagram for the synthesis of titanium dioxide nanoparticles. The GSAS suite of the Rietveld assessment tool was performed on a PAN analytical-made X-ray diffraction (XRD) diffractometer (Model EMPYREAN) using Cu Kα (λ = 1.54059 Å) radiations data. The structure was characterized using XRD results. The lattice parameters and volumes were obtained utilizing typical Rietveld refinements via software (High Score Plus). UV–vis spectrophotometer (UV cuvette with a path length of 10 mm) was used to measure the bandgap of the materials and photocatalytic degradation. The nanoparticles and their elemental analysis were analyzed using Joel-made energy-dispersive X-ray (EDX) spectroscopy (Joel 7600F, Tokyo, Japan) at 10 kV combined with the scanning electron microscope (SEM). The particle size and their related lattice plans and selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern were measured through Joel-made (JEM-120i -Transmission Electron Microscopy (TEM). To estimate the lattice characteristics, such as the spacing between atoms (d-spacing), size (volume), and Rietveld refinements were performed using new High Score Plus software. Bruker-made Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) was used to measure phase composition. Microwave absorption spectroscopy was measured by using Keysight made 1 GHz to 18 GHz using HP8722ES network analyzer. The P and X series photoluminescence (PL) spectra were acquired using a 355 nm pulsed laser with an excitation of 235 Hz along with. a cut-off filter at 370 nm.

Results and discussion Structural response X-ray diffraction (XRD) results were carried out to investigate the possible phase changes and the effect of Al and S co-substituting TiO 2  crystal structure. The XRD scanning ranges for all the samples were fixed from 20° to 80° as shown in Fig.  2 . It was observed that the Rietveld refinement with a goodness-of-fit (χ 2  = 1.70) and weighted profile factor (R WP  = 2.39%) for the pure TiO 2 , as shown in Fig.  2 a. showed a tetragonal structure along with lattice parameters i.e. a = 3.73 and b = c = 9.45. Figure  2 b shows the XRD patterns of both the P and all X series (All co-doped) nanoparticles, with structural parameters given in Table  1 . The XRD peaks for the anatase phase (AP) (JCPD-00–021-1272 card No) 50  and rutile phase (RP) (JCPD-2023- 89–4920 card No) 51  and are matched, as shown in Fig.  2 b. The absence of any additional diffraction peaks indicates that the samples do not contain any metal or non-metal ion dopants or impurities. Furthermore, the Al and S ions appear to be fully incorporated into TiO 2  fundamental structure. The AP of TiO 2  is represented by the peaks with hkl 101, 004, 200, 204, and 215 at 25.6°, 37.2°, 54.5 o , and 75.7 o  2θ angles. Moreover, the presence RP of TiO 2  is confirmed by the peaks with hkl 110, 101, 111, and 211 at 27.3°, 36.1°, 41.1°, and 54.0° 2θ angles. The mixture of APs and RPs of TiO 2  is indicated by the structure of X-series nanoparticles. The mixing of AP and RP of TiO 2  is shown by the structure of all X-series nanoparticles. It was observed that all the X-series nanoparticles show the APs and RPs of TiO 2 . Because the ionic radii of S (100 pm) and Al (143 pm), differ from those of the pure Ti (147 pm), this results in a minor peak shifting to lower 2θ (Fig.  2 c), as compared to P XRD result. This means that the doping of Al and S into the TiO 2  nanoparticles results increase in the lattice parameters as shown in Fig.  2 d Fig. 2 ( a ) The P nanoparticles XRD Rietveld refinement pattern, ( b ) represents the mixture of APs and RPs of all the XRD patterns, ( c ) the peak shift with S doping for all the samples, and ( d ) The lattice parameter with doping for all the P and X-series of nanoparticles. The inset of ( d ) shows the % of both APs and RPs of all the samples. Table 1 Structural Parameters of Al- and S-Co-doped TiO 2  Nanostructures Calculated from XRD Data. Sample (hkl) Crystallite size (nm) AP RP AP % a = b c a = b c P (101) 27.2 3.752 9.423 – – 100 ± 0.0 X1 (101) 19.7 3.761 9.431 4.579 2.94 92 ± 4.3 X2 (101) 17.9 3.772 9.454 4.562 2.93 80 ± 5.0 X3 (101) 15.4 3.783 9.461 4.621 2.97 53 ± 4.0 X4 (101) 14.5 3.795 9.484 4.620 2.92 94 ± 3.8 The lattice properties of TiO 2  are also noticeably changed by Al and S doping (Table  1 ). The lattice characteristics of TiO 2  are also significantly modified. The introduction of elements such as Al and S into the TiO 2  lattice does not completely match the size and charge of the atoms it replaces. This discrepancy creates localized distortions in the lattice structure. These distortions can develop as a result of dopants substituting Ti or O atoms or creating vacancies (missing atoms) or interstitials. These alterations in lattice structure are important due to the impact the crystal’s overall symmetry and stability. TiO 2  has two primary forms: AP (metastable) and RP (more stable). The phase transition (from AP to RP) happens when the material is heated or otherwise treated to encourage the development of rutile, which is more thermodynamically stable at higher temperatures. Dopants upset the lattice enough to facilitate the anatase phase’s conversion to rutile. The distortions created by the dopants reduce the energy needed for the phase change. This is not a contradiction, but rather a mechanism: doping helps the transition by weakening the anatase structure, allowing the material to move into the more stable rutile phase 46 . The Scherrer Eq. ( 1 ) below was used to estimate the average crystallite size for each sample 52 , 53 . 1 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
