				\begin{document}$$R \left(\%\right)=(\frac{{I}_{R}}{0.886{I}_{A}+{I}_{R}})\times 10$$\end{document} Here the IA and IR are the integrated heights of the APs and RPs, or peaks (110) and (101) in this instance, are represented by the symbols IR and IA, respectively. The variance in intensities caused by the various TiO 2  structures is taken into account by the correction factor 0.881. Figure  2 d shows the estimated relative weight fractions of the AP in the combination of the AP and RP. The AP amounts to about 87 ± 3.0% for X1 (with Al = 2% and S = 2%), whereas the calculated RP amounts to 20 ± 2.1%. Similarly, the APs weight ratio rises from 71 ± 2.0% in X2 (with Al = 2% and S = 4%) to 96 ± 9.3% in X4 (with Al = 2% and S = 8%). Pure TiO 2  contains 100% APs, whereas X1 contains 0% RPs. The proposed work demonstrated that two distinct substitutors were added to the host matrix: S 6+  and Al 2+ /Al 3+ . Depending on the valences of each element, as they have different effects. When Al 2+ /Al 3+  are added to the host matrix to replace Ti 4+ , oxygen vacancies (Ovs) are created, which prevent Ti 3+  from forming. However, as a lower valence cation it can occupy Ti 4+  locations in TiO 2 . As such, trivalent cation substituting promotes Ovs formation without producing Ti 3+  species 56 , 57 . The AP is present 100% in P, while 87 ± 3.0% of the RP is present in the X1 sample. This AP-to-RP shift is triggered by O VS  created by Al substituting. While the compound attempts to preserve charge neutrality, the addition of dopants generates an imbalanced charge and the development of vacancies. Due to this imbalance, which promotes atom dissemination, the RP is easier to form throughout the crystallization procedure. The XRD patterns demonstrate the existence of anatase and rutile phases, with rutile content increasing significantly after doping and calcination. The insertion of Al 3 ⁺/Al 2 ⁺ and S⁶⁺ ions causes lattice deformation and oxygen vacancies, resulting in a lower anatase-to-rutile transition temperature. At 500 °C, the rutile percentage rose considerably, suggesting that dopants operate as phase promoters. Following the annealing, Ti and O ions reconfigure as their bonds are disrupted, leading to the production of nanocrystals. The vacancies in X-series nanoparticles facilitate ionic diffusion, decreasing the rate of activation energy. Consequently, the development and production of the RP are increased when compared with P 58 . Recently, it was found 59  that the binding energy (BE) per unit − 21.99 eV of RP, whereas, for AP of TiO 2  is a little less at − 21.95 eV. The conversion from AP (pure) to RP includes breaking and reforming energy bonds, with a -0.043 eV transformation energy of AP (pure) unit cells. In the presence of Ovs, the BE per unit changes to − 21.75 eV for RP and − 21.72 eV for AP, respectively. Therefore, the predicted phase-change energy (PCE) for AP unit cells with Ovs converting into RP is − 0.033 eV. This reveals that Ovs can greatly reduce the PCE expected for the morphological transition, allowing AP to switch more easily to RP. Additionally, the fraction of the RP in the proposed reduces from 30 ± 2.1% to 12 ± 1.2% when the S 6+  substituting level rises from 2 to 8%. This decrease is most likely caused by the higher valence cation (S 6+ ) stabilizing Ti 3+  with no forming Ovs. Furthermore, if a pentavalent cation replaced Ti 4+  sites, it would encourage Ti 3+  production while preventing Ovs. The substitution content had a substantial impact on the transition from the anatase phase to the rutile phase, with S⁶⁺ stabilizing the anatase phase and Al 3 ⁺ promoting rutile production through enhanced lattice distortion. Higher dopant levels were found to exhibit notable saturation behavior, suggesting that there is a solubility limit inside the TiO 2  lattice beyond which additional dopants have negligible influence on phase dynamics. Additionally, it was noted that as S 6+  doping contents (varying from 2 to 8%) increase, the percentage of RP in the nanoparticles drops to 12 ± 1.2% from 30 ± 2.1%. This is explained by the fact that Ti 3+  is stabilized by the more eminent valence cation (S 6+ ) in the absence of producing Ovs. On the other hand, if a greater valence cation-like a pentavalent cation (M 5+ ) were to replace Ti 4+  sites, Ti 3+  would form instead of Ovs 60 . This claim is defended by electron spin resonance (ESR), which shows that raising the g values with S 6+  substituting, which is shown in Fig.  8 . The transition between the APs and RPs is impeded by the O VS  low density. For higher S +6  doping, rutile was found to be a minor phase and a major AP. Table  1  summarizes the retrieved D and ε values from the Williamson-Hall plots, which are shown in Figure S1. The pure TiO 2  (APs) and TiO 2  (RPs) grain sizes decreased from 27.2 to 14.5 nm, respectively, when the quantity of doping in composites increased. Doping’s function in preventing TiO 2  particle development is in line with earlier findings. It has been shown that the enhancement of photocatalytic activity is significantly influenced by both particle size and a stable phase (rutile). The SEM morphology and their corresponding elemental analysis i.e. EDX of P and X3 nanoparticles are shown in Fig.  3 a,b. This shows that the dopants are dispersed uniformly on the TiO 2  surface. Each sample showed up as a homogeneous dispersion of smaller particle agglomerates. The X series and P samples are highly similar to the previously published work and consist of regularly spherical-shaped particles that appear as distributed clusters 61 , 62 . The findings essentially match the dimension of the nanoparticles estimated from the XRD data using Eq. ( 1 ). The SEM pictures show the total regular shape, including any particle aggregation, which may lead the apparent particle size to be greater than the real crystallite size. To estimate the crystallite size, the Scherrer’s equation was applied to the XRD data, yielding an average size for the coherent scattering domain. XRD analysis confirms the nanometric dimensions of TiO 2  particles, despite apparent particle aggregation in SEM. This concordance between SEM and XRD data confirms the reported nanostructure dimensions. It must be mentioned that, for P and X3, spherical nanostructures that were formed up of several nanoparticles with a size of roughly 22–13 nm also emerged, with an average size of 19 to 10 nm, as seen in Fig.  3 ,a,b. Furthermore, the EDX analysis demonstrated the dispersion of Ti, Al, S, and O to the atomic weights as mentioned on the right side of Fig.  3 a,b, which is depicted in the EDX spectrum of the X3. The dopant values in EDX that are measured are almost the same as the values that are synthesized during synthesis. The X-series samples showed no peaks of impurities. Fig. 3 ( a  &  b )The SEM results and (right side) their corresponding elemental analysis are mentioned in the EDX for P and X3 nanoparticles. The TEM images of the P (TiO 2 ) and X3 (Al = 2% and S = 6% doped TiO 2 ) nanoparticles are displayed in Fig.  4 a,b. The P and X3 nanoparticles exhibit a homogeneous distribution of mostly spherical-shaped particles, as shown by the TEM results. The TEM analysis showed that the typical regular particle sizes are about 24 nm for P and 19 nm for X3. The reduction in particle size observed through both TEM and XRD is uniform. The inset of Fig.  4 a,b shows the SAED images for both P and X3 nanoparticles. The polycrystalline tetragonal anatase structure that makes up TiO 2  is represented by the fringes seen in the un-doped TiO 2  SAED pattern. The SAED results observed in the doped nanoparticles, following the XRD results, the presence of RP was determined by the SAED patterns seen in the TEM investigation. The diffraction ring spacings and intensity ratios matched those of rutile TiO 2 , confirming our XRD results. Thus, the SAED patterns offered additional evidence of RP development. It is essential to point out that the pronounced XRD peak observed in co-doped nanoparticles signifies a diminutive crystallite size and, consequently, an extensive extent of surface. The X3 nanoparticle sizes are greater than that of P, as seen in Fig.  3 a,b, suggesting that its surface morphology is of greater significance. Further, better photodegradation efficiency requires a greater surface area on the sample 63 , 64 . Fig. 4 ( a  &  b ) The TEM images and their corresponding SAED patterns for P and X3 (Inset of Figure ( a  &  b )). Optical response Photoluminescence measurements The PL characteristics of P and X series nanoparticles are shown in Fig.  5 a. For the P nanoparticles, there was a clear peak in emission intensity at 394 nm, which is ascribed to either the Near Band Edge emission or the release of free excitons 65 . When compared to the P nanoparticles, the PL peak in the UV region showed a significant decrease in redshifted and intensity in X-series nanoparticles. This finding suggests that the band gap of TiO 2  and the subsequent formation of electron–hole pairs can be reduced by Al/S doping 66 . The Al/S can create lattice distortions and strain in the crystal structure, affecting the electrical band arrangement and optical characteristics. This can cause modifications in the PL spectrum, such as shifts in peak location and intensity 67 . Additionally, we demonstrated that in the visible region, X series nanoparticles show extra peaks, when compared to the P nanoparticles, which show the existence of shallow trap levels that are caused by defects. When it comes to defects, generally speaking, the substitution of Al 3+  and S 6+  for Ti 4+  in TiO 2  causes a certain asymmetry of charges, which causes defects to occur as the system strives to maintain charge neutrality. Consequently, the addition of Al/S causes the formation of defect states, or mid-band gap states, which lower the band gap and serve as the electron-photogenerated center of entrapment. It was demonstrated that the defect state creation and lattice distortion brought on by Al 3 ⁺ and S⁶⁺ substitution are responsible for the observed redshift in the PL spectrum and bandgap narrowing. These processes produce mid-gap states and encourage charge carrier entrapment, which improves visible-light absorption. Based on the normalized PL spectra, the doped samples exhibited significantly lower PL intensities compared to pristine TiO 2 , indicating suppressed recombination. Although direct quantitative recombination rates were not calculated, the relative reduction (~ 35–45%) in PL intensity suggests improved charge separation. Fig. 5 ( a ) PL response for P, X1 and X3 nanoparticles. ( b ) The normal Raman spectra, ( c ) the expanded Raman spectra of P, X1, X2, X3, and X4 nanoparticles. Raman analysis A 2400 grading with a Rayleigh size 531.70 nm was used to measure the Raman spectroscopy with a scanning range of 80 to 800 cm −1  in order to study their modes of vibration, defects, phase transformation, and crystallinity. The Raman spectra of P and all X-series (co-doped TiO 2 ) nanoparticles are shown in Fig.  5 b,c. The Raman spectra of AP showed six active Raman modes, specifically (3E g  + 2B1 g  + 1A1 g ), these distinctive peaks (RP) are also observed to have four Raman characteristic modes in the extant literature 68 . Furthermore, for P nanoparticles Raman spectroscopy, no peak corresponding to Al or S is found. All the X-series nanoparticles displayed Raman peaks of RP and AP of TiO 2  compared to P nanoparticles; this is consistent with XRD measurements. In our case, five Raman modes 142 cm −1  (E g ), 381 cm −1  (B 1g ), 515 cm −1  (A 1g ), and 643 cm −1  (E g ) were observed for P nanoparticles. In contrast, extra rutile active modes were found for the X-series sample at 447 cm −1  for (E g ) and 616 cm −1  for (A 1g ) (Fig.  5 b). It is suggested that the anatase structure extension vibration is responsible for the noticeable peak that appears at 144 cm −1 69 . Furthermore, a notable shifting and broadening of Raman peaks to greater wavenumber is observed in conjunction with a reduction in peak intensity upon X-series nanoparticles, as shown in Fig.  5 c. Such behavior owing to lattice strain and distortions caused by the Al/S. These dopants modify bond lengths and angles, resulting in higher vibrational frequencies. Furthermore, the changes in the Raman and FTIR peaks show local lattice distortions brought on by the addition of dopants, which improve photocatalytic performance by providing trap states that prolong the lifetimes of charge carriers and inhibit electron–hole recombination. Furthermore, flaws and changes in the local bonding environment lead to peak widening and shifting, which indicate changes in the material vibrational and structural characteristics, which implies that the substitution of Al and S to TiO 2  breaks its symmetry and causes structural distortions 70 . These disruptions prevent TiO 2  growth and result in different defect levels, such as oxygen vacancies 71 , 72 .  Each of these elements causes a difference in the vibrational mode frequency, which broadens and shifts the Raman peaks 57 , 73 . Fourier transform infrared analysis The FTIR analysis of the P and X series nanoparticles was used in order to examine their functional groups, as shown in Fig.  6 a. For both P and X-series nanoparticles, the measurement spectrum range was selected from 500 cm −1  to 4 K cm −1 . It was observed that the FTIR spectra of all the samples illustrate the various vibrational modes. The absorption band at 520 to 1 K cm −1  can be linked to the Ti–O and Ti–O–Ti stretching modes 74 . The hydroxyl (-OH) group stretching vibration is detected at 3346 cm −1  in the spectrum. At 1640.5 cm −1  the absorption band associated with the molecular water and Ti–OH H–O–H 75 . The contribution of free hydroxyl groups is indicated by the range of 3650 to 3790 cm −1 76 . All of the peaks have a noticeable broadness because of the water that is absorbed during nanoparticle synthesis. The Ti–O and Ti–O–Ti stretching modes, which are linked to the vibrational frequencies of the TiO 2  lattice, exhibit distinctive peaks in the FTIR spectra. These same modes are also seen in the Raman spectra, where doping-induced lattice distortions cause the Ti–O and Ti–O–Ti vibrational bands to emerge with slight positional changes. The addition of dopants (Al and S) to the TiO 2  lattice and the maintenance of its general structure are further supported by the changes in the FTIR and Raman spectra. Our findings of the structural and vibrational changes brought about by the doping procedure are more reliable because of the agreement between the two approaches. Compared to P, the X-series samples moved towards a lower wavelength. It was demonstrated that the FTIR peaks in TiO 2  move to lower wavenumbers with contamination due to lattice distortions, bond weakening, and atomic mass or changes in electron density, all of which diminish vibrational frequencies 77 , 78 . Another, we also concluded that this peak shifting may also indicate that Al and S have been successfully incorporated into the TiO 2  framework. Fig. 6 ( a ) The FTIR spectra of P, X1, X2, X3 and X4. ( b ) The spectra of optical absorption and their corresponding ( c ) Tauc’s plot to calculate the ( d ) band gap value between P and X series nanoparticles. UV–Vis The UV–Vis reflectance spectra in the 200–800 nm range were used to analyze the optical characteristics of both bare and P and X series nanoparticles, as displayed in Fig.  6 b,c. It was observed that in comparison to P, all X-series nanoparticles exhibited redshift at a wavelength of about 387 nm as the S concentration of the dopants increased, demonstrating that the optical edges of absorption of X-series nanoparticles moved towards the lower wavelength in comparison to P nanoparticles. The response is due to the doping of S, which presents its energy level close to the conduction band, thereby enhancing the TiO 2  ability to absorb visible light 57 , 79 . Furthermore, Al 2+/3+  is important because it lowers the band gap of TiO 2  and reduces defect levels 80 . To further confirm the band of both the P and X series nanoparticles, the Tauc relation ( 3 ) 81 , was used to calculate the band gap of the P and X series nanoparticles as shown in Fig.  6 b. 3 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
