p orbitals of O and Ti in the TiO 2  lattice make up the VB and CB. The crystal field model divides the bottom and upper sections of the CB into two separate components because the 3d orbital of Ti divides into two separate components of the t2g and eg, octahedral field states. Whenever Al/S is introduced to TiO 2  lattice, the 3p states S play an important role in building the VB with 2p and 3d states of O and Ti; following which, the 3p states S become delocalized. As the co-dopant is induced to the TiO 2  lattice, the 3p states of S become delocalized, which contributes significantly to the formation of the O 2p and Ti 3d states in the VB 108 , 109 . Therefore, the 3p states of S are combined with VB to expand it, compensating for the drop in band gap caused by inserting impurity into the TiO 2  matrix and thereby boosting methylene blue photodegradation. (iii) Greater S concentration led to a greater degradation in X series nanoparticles, indicating a greater percentage of AP in the AR phase combination (Fig.  2 b–d). The physical process of AP generation with S 6+  is thoroughly addressed in the XRD part. Som and his co-workers used computational calculations by visible-light first principles to investigate inter-band shifts in various phases of TiO 2 110 , 111 . Their observations revealed that RP and brookite have direct band gaps, whereas, AP has an indirect band gap induced by phonon-assisted coupling of photoexcited electrons and holes. AP has a greater lifespan for photogenerated electron–hole pairs compared to RP and brookite due to its preventing excited electrons and holes from recombining directly. AP indirect band gap allows higher diffusion durations and quicker responses for excited electrons and holes. Furthermore, the standard effective weight of photogenerated holes and electrons in AP is lower compared to that in RP and brookite, allowing greater and more effective movement and transportation of photoexcited carriers of charge from inside to the outer layer for use in photocatalytic processes. In addition, the major functional facet of RP (110) has a small energy at the surface, which reduces molecule adsorption and disintegration. These elements work together to explain why AP generally outperforms RP and brookite in terms of photocatalytic efficiency 112 , 113 . The rates of photocatalytic degradation correlated with diluted dye conditions and followed a pseudo-first-order model. But in order to investigate adsorption-reaction mechanisms in more intricate systems, further studies may use the Langmuir–Hinshelwood model. The reusability of the doped TiO 2  catalyst was assessed over three successive photocatalytic degradation cycles of methylene blue. The catalyst retained more than 90% of its original degradation efficiency, indicating excellent stability and reusability. The minimal activity loss suggests strong structural integrity and resistance to photocorrosion during operation. Although this work focused on methylene blue, the doped TiO 2  system’s bandgap narrowing and oxygen vacancy creation indicate significant reactivity toward a wider variety of contaminants, including phenol and rhodamine B. The production of reactive oxygen species (·OH and O 2 ·⁻) suggests potential efficacy in decomposing aromatic and azo compounds, requiring more application-specific research. Mechanism of photodergartion of P and X series of photocatalysts under sunlight irradiation The possible mechanism of methylene blue degradation upon exposure to X-series nanoparticles by visible light irradiation is illustrated in Fig.  9 . Specifically, the addition of Al and S to TiO 2  has resulted in a notable increase in the material’s photocatalytic efficiency by moving its bandgap to the visible region. This behavior (shift) is explained by the existence of induced defects and Al 2+ /Al 3+  and S 6+  ions in the TiO 2  lattice. Together, these elements create donor energy levels that are shallow in the TiO 2  forbidden region. After the X series nanoparticles are subjected to the visible spectrum, absorption moves the excited charge particles in the VB to these defect energy states and CB. These charged electrons are easily grabbed by defects and substituted ions. The separation of charges occurs within the photoexcited electron and the corresponding hole of the VB in TiO 2  nanoparticles. The photo-exposed electron–hole pairs can be effectively used for a variety of photocatalytic reactions thanks to this charge separation. When the photoexcited electrons come into contact with oxygen (from dissolved O 2 ), they can reduce to a single electron, producing extremely responsive transitory ions like the superoxide anion radical anion O 2 ·. Similar to this, when water interacts with the electron holes (h + ) in the VB, OH· are created (single electron oxidation). Because of the exceptional reactivity of these active radicals, methylene blue molecules can be attacked, causing the substance to either entirely convert into water (H 2 O), carbon dioxide (CO 2 ) and or decompose into more minor parts as shown in Fig.  9 114 . TiO 2  is used in environmental remediation, such as wastewater treatment, air purification, and self-cleaning surfaces, due to its mechanism. The co-doped TiO 2  nanoparticles use photocatalytic processes to reduce pollutants and promote cleaner habitats. Fig. 9 Mechanism approach of Al and S co-doped TiO 2  photocatalytic degradation process. The co-doped TiO 2  nanoparticles’ continuous photocatalytic activity over five cycles was shown by reusability experiments, indicating structural stability. To measure long-term environmental performance, more testing is recommended in actual sunlight and wastewater environments. Although future XPS and UPS studies would offer direct evidence of the electronic structure modifications in this work. However, the observed redshift in absorption edge in the UV–Vis spectra, along with PL emission quenching and changes in optical band gap values, indicate significant alterations in the band structure due to Al 3 ⁺/Al 2 ⁺ and S⁶⁺ co-doping. These optical characterizations provide a solid basis for interpreting the improved charge separation and visible-light response.

