ing from 2 to 8%) increase, the percentage of RP in the nanoparticles drops to 12 ± 1.2% from 30 ± 2.1%. This is explained by the fact that Ti 3+  is stabilized by the more eminent valence cation (S 6+ ) in the absence of producing Ovs. On the other hand, if a greater valence cation-like a pentavalent cation (M 5+ ) were to replace Ti 4+  sites, Ti 3+  would form instead of Ovs 60 . This claim is defended by electron spin resonance (ESR), which shows that raising the g values with S 6+  substituting, which is shown in Fig.  8 . The transition between the APs and RPs is impeded by the O VS  low density. For higher S +6  doping, rutile was found to be a minor phase and a major AP. Table  1  summarizes the retrieved D and ε values from the Williamson-Hall plots, which are shown in Figure S1. The pure TiO 2  (APs) and TiO 2  (RPs) grain sizes decreased from 27.2 to 14.5 nm, respectively, when the quantity of doping in composites increased. Doping’s function in preventing TiO 2  particle development is in line with earlier findings. It has been shown that the enhancement of photocatalytic activity is significantly influenced by both particle size and a stable phase (rutile). The SEM morphology and their corresponding elemental analysis i.e. EDX of P and X3 nanoparticles are shown in Fig.  3 a,b. This shows that the dopants are dispersed uniformly on the TiO 2  surface. Each sample showed up as a homogeneous dispersion of smaller particle agglomerates. The X series and P samples are highly similar to the previously published work and consist of regularly spherical-shaped particles that appear as distributed clusters 61 , 62 . The findings essentially match the dimension of the nanoparticles estimated from the XRD data using Eq. ( 1 ). The SEM pictures show the total regular shape, including any particle aggregation, which may lead the apparent particle size to be greater than the real crystallite size. To estimate the crystallite size, the Scherrer’s equation was applied 