mance by providing trap states that prolong the lifetimes of charge carriers and inhibit electron–hole recombination. Furthermore, flaws and changes in the local bonding environment lead to peak widening and shifting, which indicate changes in the material vibrational and structural characteristics, which implies that the substitution of Al and S to TiO 2  breaks its symmetry and causes structural distortions 70 . These disruptions prevent TiO 2  growth and result in different defect levels, such as oxygen vacancies 71 , 72 .  Each of these elements causes a difference in the vibrational mode frequency, which broadens and shifts the Raman peaks 57 , 73 . Fourier transform infrared analysis The FTIR analysis of the P and X series nanoparticles was used in order to examine their functional groups, as shown in Fig.  6 a. For both P and X-series nanoparticles, the measurement spectrum range was selected from 500 cm −1  to 4 K cm −1 . It was observed that the FTIR spectra of all the samples illustrate the various vibrational modes. The absorption band at 520 to 1 K cm −1  can be linked to the Ti–O and Ti–O–Ti stretching modes 74 . The hydroxyl (-OH) group stretching vibration is detected at 3346 cm −1  in the spectrum. At 1640.5 cm −1  the absorption band associated with the molecular water and Ti–OH H–O–H 75 . The contribution of free hydroxyl groups is indicated by the range of 3650 to 3790 cm −1 76 . All of the peaks have a noticeable broadness because of the water that is absorbed during nanoparticle synthesis. The Ti–O and Ti–O–Ti stretching modes, which are linked to the vibrational frequencies of the TiO 2  lattice, exhibit distinctive peaks in the FTIR spectra. These same modes are also seen in the Raman spectra, where doping-induced lattice distortions cause the Ti–O and Ti–O–Ti vibrational bands to emerge with slight positional changes. The addition of dopants (Al and S) to the TiO 2  lattice and the maintenance of its general structure are further supported by