Electron spin resonance response The ESR is a technique that uses unpaired electrons in atoms or ions to absorb microwave radiation. At a specific frequency, electron magnetic moment interactions with magnetic fields are studied by ESR. The Zeeman Effect splits photon levels inside an atom into many lines when exposed to an external magnetic field. As a result, ESR focuses on the immediate change across electronic Zeeman states 82 , 83 . For the defect centers investigation in P and X series samples, a SPINSCAN-made TE102 (ADANI SPINSCAN X spectrometer was used. Figure  7 a,b displays the ESR spectrum of the P and X-series nanoparticles at 300 K. The simulations were performed by altering the magnetic field intensity from 280 to 380 mT. The g-valve values determined from ESR spectra from 1.990 to 1.990 for P and X series nanoparticles, indicating O or Ti 3+  vacancies on the surface. The estimated g-valves in Table  3  for all the nanoparticles were P = 1.991, X1 = 1.990, X2 = 1.984, X3 = 1.985 and X4 = 1.988 by utilizing the following Eq. ( 4 ) 84 , 4 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
