nanopartciles. Sample S (%) g-value Line width ΔH (G) P 0 1.991 2.04 X1 2 1.990 4.02 X2 4 1.984 5.16 X3 6 1.985 0.4 X4 8 1.988 1.13 The line width (ΔH) for samples with all S contents is determined using the Rubbins and Bleanny relation 89 – 91  and is shown in Table  3 . It was demonstrated that the intricate interplay involving spin–spin interactions, defect states, and magnetic disorder within the samples might be the reason for the mentioned increase and decrease in the ESR spectra ΔH as a function of S content 92 , 93 . First, the ΔH broadens when the S content rises to 4% (X2 sample) because of stronger spin–spin interactions and increased dipolar coupling, which are probably brought about by the addition of additional paramagnetic sites and localized spins. The larger resonance signal is the result of this increase in instability and coupling intensity. On the other hand, the ΔH falls when the S substitution approaches 6% (X3 sample), indicating an eventual decrease in spin–spin coupling 94 . This might result from a reduction in localized defect states via compensatory effects or from the development of clusters or spin pairs, which lower the net magnetic moment leading to the ESR signal 95 . The ΔH rises again with greater S contents, such as 8% substituting (X4 sample). Enhanced spin couplings and wider lines are probably caused by increased defect density and the return of magnetic disorder. Thus, when the S doping level fluctuates, the entire behavior represents the conflicting impacts of disorder, magnetic, or spin coupling. By acting as electron traps and facilitating longer electron lifetimes and improved charge separation, the Ti 3 ⁺-Ovs complexes seen in the ESR spectra increase the production of ROS during photocatalysis. Additionally, the observed ESR signals are consistent with their paramagnetic nature, suggesting defect-induced magnetic behavior.

Photocatalytic activity Photodegradation of MB dye over P and X(X1,X3, and X4) Nanostructures The methylene blue (MB) (or dye) was used as a reference to measure the photocatalytic degradation responses of the P and X series (X1, X3, and X4) when exposed to visible light as shown in Fig.  8 . A halogen lamp of 200 W as a light power was utilized to radiate the MB dye (as a pollutant)added nanoparticles. Almost 40 mg of nanoparticles were mixed into a 100 ml solution containing a dye concentration of 30 mg/L. To establish an adsorption–desorption balance on the photocatalytic surface, the nanoparticles in the solution were exposed to darkness for one hour. After that, the solution was left in the light and constantly stirred. Figure  8 a shows the absorbance of the P nanoparticles solution that contains methylene blue (MB) dye versus periods (for 0, 15, 30, 60, 90, 120, and 150 min). The absorption peak intensity of the MB dye solution was shown to hardly decrease (11.1% in 150 min) as the irradiation time increased, suggesting that dye degradation occurs much more slowly in the presence of P nanoparticles. A very small amount of decrease in the absorption peak intensity of the methylene blue dye solution (11.1% reduction in 150 min) suggested that dye degradation happens substantially slower in P nanoparticles due to their restricted photocatalytic activity under the present circumstances. P nanoparticle has a broad bandgap, making it less effective in visible light, which generates fewer charge carriers. Furthermore, the quick recombination of electron–hole pairs, along with possibly poor surface area, pore volumes, and pore size 96 , reduces its catalytic efficacy in dye degradation. Lacking dopants or co-catalysts to boost operation, the degradation mechanism is considerably retarded. Conversely, compared to P nanocrystals, all three doped nanocrystals samples (X1, X3, and X4) exhibit greater rates of photocatalysis and photodegradation, as shown in Fig.  8 b–d. Doping TiO 2  with Al and S creates additional energy levels in the bandgap, allowing it to absorb more light, including visible light. In contrast, pristine TiO 2  absorbs UV light due to its large bandgap. As a consequence, the doped samples (X1, X3, and X4) use solar energy more efficiently, resulting in the formation of more electron–hole pairs (PEHPs) that drive photocatalytic processes. One notable constraint of TiO 2  photocatalysis is the fast recombination of electron–hole pairs produced by UV light. Doping with Al and S reduces this recombination by producing defect states that can capture photogenerated electrons or holes. This increased charge separation enables a more effective photocatalytic process because electrons and holes are accessible for oxidation and reduction processes rather than recombination. Combining Al and S doping in TiO 2  generates a synergistic effect that improves photocatalytic capabilities. While Al doping adds electron-donating characteristics, S doping reduces recombination rates and changes the bandgap location to improve electron transport. This combined effect outperforms single doping with either Al or S, resulting in higher photocatalytic activity and quicker photodegradation rates for the doped samples.More precisely, the intensity of the absorption peak decreased sharply with exposure duration for the X-series nanoparticles, and the majority of the organic dye molecules disintegrated in less than 140 min of exposure. Fig. 8 The Degradation of MB loaded with ( a ) P, the Degradation of MB photocatalysis of ( b ) X1, ( c ) X3, and ( d ) X4. ( e ) The  \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
