n. After the X series nanoparticles are subjected to the visible spectrum, absorption moves the excited charge particles in the VB to these defect energy states and CB. These charged electrons are easily grabbed by defects and substituted ions. The separation of charges occurs within the photoexcited electron and the corresponding hole of the VB in TiO 2  nanoparticles. The photo-exposed electron–hole pairs can be effectively used for a variety of photocatalytic reactions thanks to this charge separation. When the photoexcited electrons come into contact with oxygen (from dissolved O 2 ), they can reduce to a single electron, producing extremely responsive transitory ions like the superoxide anion radical anion O 2 ·. Similar to this, when water interacts with the electron holes (h + ) in the VB, OH· are created (single electron oxidation). Because of the exceptional reactivity of these active radicals, methylene blue molecules can be attacked, causing the substance to either entirely convert into water (H 2 O), carbon dioxide (CO 2 ) and or decompose into more minor parts as shown in Fig.  9 114 . TiO 2  is used in environmental remediation, such as wastewater treatment, air purification, and self-cleaning surfaces, due to its mechanism. The co-doped TiO 2  nanoparticles use photocatalytic processes to reduce pollutants and promote cleaner habitats. Fig. 9 Mechanism approach of Al and S co-doped TiO 2  photocatalytic degradation process. The co-doped TiO 2  nanoparticles’ continuous photocatalytic activity over five cycles was shown by reusability experiments, indicating structural stability. To measure long-term environmental performance, more testing is recommended in actual sunlight and wastewater environments. Although future XPS and UPS studies would offer direct evidence of the electronic structure modifications in this work. However, the observed redshift in absorption edge in the UV–Vis spectra, along with PL emission quenching and changes in optical band gap values, indicate significant alterations in the band structure due to Al 3 ⁺/Al 2 ⁺ and S⁶⁺ co-doping. These optical characterizations provide a solid basis for interpreting the improved charge separation and visible-light response.

