d S creates additional energy levels in the bandgap, allowing it to absorb more light, including visible light. In contrast, pristine TiO 2  absorbs UV light due to its large bandgap. As a consequence, the doped samples (X1, X3, and X4) use solar energy more efficiently, resulting in the formation of more electron–hole pairs (PEHPs) that drive photocatalytic processes. One notable constraint of TiO 2  photocatalysis is the fast recombination of electron–hole pairs produced by UV light. Doping with Al and S reduces this recombination by producing defect states that can capture photogenerated electrons or holes. This increased charge separation enables a more effective photocatalytic process because electrons and holes are accessible for oxidation and reduction processes rather than recombination. Combining Al and S doping in TiO 2  generates a synergistic effect that improves photocatalytic capabilities. While Al doping adds electron-donating characteristics, S doping reduces recombination rates and changes the bandgap location to improve electron transport. This combined effect outperforms single doping with either Al or S, resulting in higher photocatalytic activity and quicker photodegradation rates for the doped samples.More precisely, the intensity of the absorption peak decreased sharply with exposure duration for the X-series nanoparticles, and the majority of the organic dye molecules disintegrated in less than 140 min of exposure. Fig. 8 The Degradation of MB loaded with ( a ) P, the Degradation of MB photocatalysis of ( b ) X1, ( c ) X3, and ( d ) X4. ( e ) The  \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
