Mechanism of photodergartion of P and X series of photocatalysts under sunlight irradiation The possible mechanism of methylene blue degradation upon exposure to X-series nanoparticles by visible light irradiation is illustrated in Fig.  9 . Specifically, the addition of Al and S to TiO 2  has resulted in a notable increase in the material’s photocatalytic efficiency by moving its bandgap to the visible region. This behavior (shift) is explained by the existence of induced defects and Al 2+ /Al 3+  and S 6+  ions in the TiO 2  lattice. Together, these elements create donor energy levels that are shallow in the TiO 2  forbidden region. After the X series nanoparticles are subjected to the visible spectrum, absorption moves the excited charge particles in the VB to these defect energy states and CB. These charged electrons are easily grabbed by defects and substituted ions. The separation of charges occurs within the photoexcited electron and the corresponding hole of the VB in TiO 2  nanoparticles. The photo-exposed electron–hole pairs can be effectively used for a variety of photocatalytic reactions thanks to this charge separation. When the photoexcited electrons come into contact with oxygen (from dissolved O 2 ), they can reduce to a single electron, producing extremely responsive transitory ions like the superoxide anion radical anion O 2 ·. Similar to this, when water interacts with the electron holes (h + ) in the VB, OH· are created (single electron oxidation). Because of the exceptional reactivity of these active radicals, methylene blue molecules can be attacked, causing the substance to either entirely convert into water (H 2 O), carbon dioxide (CO 2 ) and or decompose into more minor parts as shown in Fig.  9 114 . TiO 2  is used in environmental remediation, such as wastewater treatment, air purification, and self-cleaning surfaces, due to its mechanism. The co-doped TiO 2  nanoparticles use photocatalytic processes to reduce pollutants and promote cleaner habitats. Fig. 9 Mechanism approach of Al and S co-doped TiO 2  photocatalytic degradation process. The co-doped TiO 2  nanoparticles’ continuous photocatalytic activity over five cycles was shown by reusability experiments, indicating structural stability. To measure long-term environmental performance, more testing is recommended in actual sunlight and wastewater environments. Although future XPS and UPS studies would offer direct evidence of the electronic structure modifications in this work. However, the observed redshift in absorption edge in the UV–Vis spectra, along with PL emission quenching and changes in optical band gap values, indicate significant alterations in the band structure due to Al 3 ⁺/Al 2 ⁺ and S⁶⁺ co-doping. These optical characterizations provide a solid basis for interpreting the improved charge separation and visible-light response.

Conclusion This work found that co-doping TiO 2  nanoparticles with Al 3 ⁺ and S⁶⁺ had a considerable influence on their structural, optical, and photocatalytic capabilities. The introduction of these dopants was discovered to have an important role in modulating the anatase-to-rutile phase transition, principally by creating oxygen vacancies that reduce the energy barrier for this transformation. The XRD examination confirms that larger concentrations of S⁶⁺ enhance the stability of the anatase phase, whereas Al 3 ⁺ promotes the production of the rutile phase. The study found that doped nanoparticles had a more uniform size distribution and higher crystallinity than undoped TiO 2 . Optical testing, including photoluminescence and UV–Vis spectroscopy, revealed that the addition of Al and S dopants introduces mid-gap states and narrows the band gap, expanding the light absorption range into the visible spectrum. ESR study reveals paramagnetic centers in Ti 3 ⁺-Ovs complexes, indicating defect-induced magnetic characteristics. This alteration improves TiO 2  photocatalytic activity, as indicated by a substantial increase in methylene blue degradation rates when exposed to visible light. Kinetic tests confirmed these findings, with co-doped samples demonstrating much higher rate constants for dye degradation, indicating greater efficiency in using visible light for photocatalysis. The redshift in the absorption edges and reduction in photoluminescence intensity indicate that the co-doping technique efficiently reduces electron–hole recombination, improving overall photocatalytic performance. This method not only stabilizes the anatase phase at higher co-doping but also considerably increases the materials response to visible light, making it an extremely intriguing option for environmental applications such as pollution degradation. Future research might look at optimizing dopant concentrations and the potential of this material in real-world photocatalytic systems, broadening its application breadth.

